  "c053826c-a233-49b1-9804-b6658ccf0b71": {
   "id": "c053826c-a233-49b1-9804-b6658ccf0b71",
   "message": {
    "id": "c053826c-a233-49b1-9804-b6658ccf0b71",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1786599179.483,
    "update_time": 1786599182.4035652,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "> **Privacy and surveillance.** Instructor-facing telemetry can easily become surveillance. TutorTrace collected data only from students who opted in (87 of 96; nine declined and were never recorded), hashed all keystrokes outside code files, and showed TAs pseudonymous IDs only. We designed TutorTrace to help TAs decide *whom to visit*, not to evaluate students, and it should not be used for grading. Still, the same replay that helps a TA notice a stalled student could be used to monitor effort or penalize idle time. Deployments should make the purpose explicit to students, limit retention to the course term, and give students a way to view their own timeline. Tools in this space should be judged not only by how well they detect struggle but by how they shift the power relationship between students and course staff."
     ]
    },
    "status": "finished_successfully",
